#HowToWork?

You will require the Github Token. Proceed as follows:

Step 1: Open token settings

1. Sign in to GitHub.
2. Click your profile picture (top right), then Settings.
3. In the left sidebar, scroll to the bottom and click Developer settings.
4. Click Personal access tokens, then Fine-grained tokens.
5. Click Generate new token.
Step 2: Fill in the token form

Token name: something like kolosok-rehack-local.
Expiration: choose a date. 90 days is a reasonable default.
Resource owner: select Oleks0ir.
Repository access: choose Only select repositories, then pick kolosok-rehack.
Permissions: expand Repository permissions, find Contents, and set it to Read and write. Leave everything else at “No access”.
Click Generate token.

 
Step 2: Fill in the token form

1. Token name: something like kolosok-rehack-local.
2. Expiration: choose a date. 90 days is a reasonable default.
3. Resource owner: select <YOUR NAME>
4. Repository access: choose Only select repositories, then pick kolosok-rehack.
5. Permissions: expand Repository permissions, find Contents, and set it to Read and write. Leave everything else at “No access”.
6. Click Generate token.

>IMPORTANT COPY THE TOKEN AS IT WILL DISAPPEAR

Step 3: 

Step 4: Clear the old saved password
Open a PowerShell window and run:

git credential-manager erase

If that command isn’t found, open Windows Credential Manager instead (Start menu → search for it), go to Windows Credentials, find the entry for git:https://github.com, and remove it.

Step 5: Retry your Git command
Run the command that failed, for example:

git push

When Git asks for credentials:

Username: <YOUR USERNAME>
Password: paste the token (github_pat_...), not your GitHub password

Git Credential Manager saves the token, so you should only need to do this once until the token expires.

